{"task_id": "Mbpp/239", "solution": "def get_total_number_of_sequences(m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the number of sequences of length n, such that each element is a positive integer <= m,\n    and each element is at least twice the previous one.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 1:\n        return m\n\n    # current[v] stores the number of sequences of the current length ending with value v.\n    # Arrays are 1-indexed for values (1 to m), so size m+1.\n    current = [0] * (m + 1)\n    for v in range(1, m + 1):\n        current[v] = 1  # For length 1, any value from 1 to m is a valid sequence.\n\n    for length in range(2, n + 1):\n        # prefix_sum[k] stores the sum of current[1] through current[k].\n        prefix_sum = [0] * (m + 1)\n        running_sum = 0\n        for i in range(1, m + 1):\n            running_sum += current[i]\n            prefix_sum[i] = running_sum\n\n        next_current = [0] * (m + 1)\n        for v in range(1, m + 1):\n            # To form a sequence ending with 'v', the previous element 'prev_v' must satisfy 2 * prev_v <= v.\n            # This implies prev_v <= v // 2. We sum counts for all such valid previous elements.\n            max_prev_v = v // 2\n            if max_prev_v >= 1:\n                next_current[v] = prefix_sum[max_prev_v]\n            else:\n                next_current[v] = 0\n        current = next_current\n\n    # The final answer is the sum of all sequences of length n ending at any value from 1 to m.\n    return sum(current[1:])"}
